# AN INSTRUMENT, NEVER THE PRODUCT: WHAT THE INTERPOLATION PASS HOLDS OF OPTICAL FLOW AT A PIXEL, AS NUMBERS IN THE PICTURE (2026-10-11;
# the torn bottom of the tyre, docs\reports\fg-3\desk-2026-10-11-ghost\env). Every pixel of the generated image comes
# out as three numbers a replay can read back (tools\test-fg-own\tyre.py):
#   red, green   optical flow's half vector at the pixel, in pixels of the display (0 0 where it found none)
#   blue         the share the pass gives the colour fetched along the GAME's vector (1: all of it; 0: the colour
#                fetched along optical flow's vector alone)
# and nothing is inpainted, so that the numbers stand.
what AN INSTRUMENT (the picture changes): every pixel is painted with optical flow's half vector (display pixels) and the share of the game's colour against optical flow's
passes ffx_frameinterpolation_pass
wave64 the same edit in both variants: the pass has no wave operation
file framegeneration/fsr3/include/gpu/frameinterpolation/ffx_frameinterpolation.h
--- old
        fInterpolatedColor = ffxLerp(ofColor, fInterpolatedColor, ffxSaturate(fGameMvBias));
--- new
        fInterpolatedColor = ffxLerp(ofColor, fInterpolatedColor, ffxSaturate(fGameMvBias));
        // (the instrument v-show-flow)
        fInterpolatedColor = FfxFloat32x3(ofMv.fMotionVector * fUvLetterBoxScale * FfxFloat32x2(DisplaySize()), ffxSaturate(fGameMvBias));
        fInPaintingWeight  = 0.0f;
--- end
